---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const asideLinks = [
  { label: 'How guides are built', href: '#how-guides-are-built' },
  { label: 'Risk levels', href: '#risk-levels' },
  { label: 'What we do not do', href: '#what-we-do-not-do' },
  { label: 'Using a guide safely', href: '#using-a-guide-safely' },
  { label: 'Corrections and feedback', href: '#corrections-and-feedback' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="How FixProblemNow works"
  metaTitle="How It Works: Our Troubleshooting Method"
  description="See how FixProblemNow researches, writes, orders and reviews troubleshooting guides, how risk levels work, and what the site cannot do."
  canonical="/how-it-works/"
  eyebrow="Our method"
  intro="FixProblemNow is a reference library, not a remote repair service. Here is how a guide is put together, how the steps are ordered, and where the limits are."
  asideLinks={asideLinks}
  asideNote="If a guide is unclear or a step is wrong for your model, tell us. Corrections are part of the process."
>
  <h2 id="what-this-site-is">What this site is</h2>
  <p>
    FixProblemNow publishes independent troubleshooting guides for everyday technology: computers,
    phones, home networks, printers, email, software, televisions, streaming devices, peripherals,
    storage and accounts. The aim is to help you identify the most likely cause, try the safest
    checks first, and recognise the point where a repair or account recovery process needs someone
    else.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site does not connect to your device, run scans, request remote access, or require an
    account. Every published guide is readable without signing in. Manufacturer names are used to
    describe compatibility and to point to official tools; {site.name} is not affiliated with those
    companies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-guides-are-built">How guides are built</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Start with a specific symptom.</strong>
      <p>
        A guide is written for one recognisable problem, such as a laptop that shows no lights or a
        printer that stays offline while other devices work. Broad topics are split so the steps do
        not contradict each other.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the supported tools and menus.</strong>
      <p>
        Steps are checked against current manufacturer documentation and operating-system support
        pages. Where Windows 10 and Windows 11 differ, or where Android manufacturers use different
        labels, the guide says so rather than pretending one menu fits every device.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Order steps by risk, not by cleverness.</strong>
      <p>
        Reversible checks come first: cables, power, connections, restarts, and built-in
        troubleshooters. Destructive actions such as clearing app data, resetting a device, or
        erasing a disk are placed later with a warning and a backup requirement.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add verification and a stopping point.</strong>
      <p>
        Each guide explains how to confirm that the problem is actually fixed and when to stop
        before making things worse. A page that only says “try these ten things” without either is
        not finished.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Review links, sources and dates.</strong>
      <p>
        Official documentation is linked in the guide. Publication and update dates come from the
        article metadata; we do not stagger dates to look fresher. A correction changes the update
        date.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <p class="callout__label">Transparency</p>
    <p>
      We do not claim to have physically tested every device, app version, or router model named on
      the site. Guides combine documented behaviour, standard troubleshooting practice, and
      safety review. Model-specific behaviour can still differ, which is why each guide includes a
      “when to stop” section and links to the manufacturer’s own instructions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="risk-levels">Risk levels</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide carries a risk label. The label describes the chance of data loss, hardware damage,
    or a change that is difficult to undo if a step is followed incorrectly.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Level</th>
          <th scope="col">What it means</th>
          <th scope="col">Examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="badge badge--risk-low">Low</span></td>
          <td>Reversible checks that should not remove files or change hardware.</td>
          <td>Restarting a router, reseating a cable, clearing an app cache.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="badge badge--risk-moderate">Moderate</span></td>
          <td>Settings or repairs that can remove local data or need care to reverse.</td>
          <td>Reinstalling a driver, resetting network settings, removing an app.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="badge badge--risk-high">High</span></td>
          <td>Destructive or hardware-adjacent steps where a mistake can cause data loss or injury.</td>
          <td>Erasing a disk, opening a device, replacing a battery, editing firmware.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Risk labels are guidance, not a guarantee. A low-risk step can still cause a problem on a
    damaged device. Stop if you see smoke, smell burning, notice swelling, or find liquid inside
    equipment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-we-do-not-do">What we do not do</h2>
  <ul class="icon-list">
    <li>
      <Icon name="close" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>We do not remotely access devices.</strong>
        <span>No guide asks you to install a remote-control tool or give a stranger access to your computer.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <Icon name="close" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>We do not recover accounts.</strong>
        <span>Password, two-factor and account recovery must go through the provider that controls the account.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <Icon name="close" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>We do not sell repairs or parts.</strong>
        <span>There are no repair bookings, parts sales, or paid support calls on this site.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <Icon name="close" size={18} />
      <div>
        <strong>We do not guarantee an outcome.</strong>
        <span>Hardware faults, account locks and provider outages can be outside anyone’s remote control.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="using-a-guide-safely">Using a guide safely</h2>
  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Read the whole guide before changing anything, especially the safety notes.</li>
    <li>Back up important files before a reset, reinstall, disk repair, or storage change.</li>
    <li>Change one thing at a time so you can tell which step made a difference.</li>
    <li>Record the original setting before you change it, including network names and DNS values.</li>
    <li>Use official manufacturer downloads and support pages rather than unofficial driver bundles.</li>
    <li>Stop when a step feels unsafe, when a device heats up unusually, or when data is at risk.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="corrections-and-feedback">Corrections and feedback</h2>
  <p>
    Each guide includes a feedback control and a link to report an issue. If a menu has moved, a
    command has changed, or a step is wrong for your model, use the
    <a href="/contact/?topic=correction">contact form</a> and include the guide URL, device model,
    software version, and what you saw. We cannot promise an individual reply time, but correction
    reports are reviewed and the guide is updated when the problem is confirmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full process is described in our <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> and
    <a href="/corrections-policy/">corrections policy</a>.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
